  "attributionNote": "Attributed by tradition to the teacher Shvetashvatara, who is named in the last chapter. The text is the work of a theistic circle within the Vedic schools.",
  "description": "The Shvetashvatara Upanishad teaches a personal Lord who is the cause of the world and the self within every being: it describes the one who pervades all things, the wheel of becoming with its one and its many, the powers of nature and the individual soul that depend on him, and the disciplines — truth, austerity, meditation and the grace of the Lord — by which he is known. It is the Upanishad in which the yoga of meditation and the worship of a supreme Lord meet.",
  "historicalContext": "One of the later principal Upanishads, it was important to the devotional Vedanta of Ramanuja and Madhva and is quoted for its description of the Lord and for its verses on yoga and the mind as the lamp of a steady flame.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: the six chapters of the Sanskrit text as GRETIL publishes it, streamed from GRETIL at read time, one chapter at a time. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
